<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Nangchen</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1932 CE (approx.) · capital Nangchen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of nomad and farming clans in the upper Mekong and Yangtze valleys of Kham, famous for its many Kagyu monasteries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Nangchen, in today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yushu-tibetan-autonomous-prefecture">Yushu</a> region of southern Qinghai, emerged as a principality under a hereditary royal family in the Mongol period. Tradition credits its kings with receiving recognition from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sakya-regime">Sakya</a> lama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogon-chogyal-phagpa">Phagpa</a> in the 13th century, and their house ruled a federation of local tribes from the royal seat at Nangchen.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king presided over some twenty-five tribes, mostly yak-herding nomads with some farming villages, each under its own chief. The kingdom was a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drukpa-kagyu">Drukpa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barom-kagyu">Barom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drigung-kagyu">Drigung Kagyu</a> traditions, with dozens of monasteries. From the 18th century it was nominally under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> supervision from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xining">Xining</a>, while maintaining close religious ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the Sino-Tibetan fighting of 1932 the Muslim warlords of Qinghai took effective control, and Nangchen's autonomy ended. The royal family kept some local standing until the 1950s, when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> abolished the old order.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nangchen.jpg" alt="Nangchen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikiwakhan · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Gar_monastery_(Ga%27er_in_Chinese),_Nangq%C3%AAn_county.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1932 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nangchen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism (Kagyu)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Nangchen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1616&amp;focus=nangchen" aria-label="Nangchen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7153 1004 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nangchen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7473 1260L7567 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7473 1260h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7473 1260h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1616&amp;focus=nangchen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

